    {
      "op": "replace",
      "path": "/spec/definitions/@D023/definition",
      "value": "For smooth mean-zero initial data and tau=T/L>0, on I_n=[t_n,t_(n+1)] put f_n=v^n, F_n(t)=Stage_n(t;f_n), z_n(t)=f_n+F_n(t), C_n(t)=integral_(t_n)^t Bop_s(f_n,F_n(s)) ds, Q_n(t)=(1/2)integral_(t_n)^t Bop_s(F_n(s),F_n(s)) ds, W_n=Q_n(t_(n+1))+Remainder_(t_n,tau)(f_n,f_n,f_n), V(t)=f_n+F_n(t)+C_n(t)+Q_n(t)-(t-t_n)*W_n/tau, and Y_n(t)=V(t)-z_n(t). Put rho_n(t)=-integral_(t_n)^t Bop_s(Y_n,z_n) ds-(1/2)integral_(t_n)^t Bop_s(Y_n,Y_n) ds-(t-t_n)*W_n/tau. Define Rres(0)=0 and concatenate rho_n: Rres(t)=sum_(j<n)rho_j(t_(j+1))+rho_n(t) on I_n. For an exact twisted solution v, set e=v-V, delta_n=||f_n-v(t_n)||_2, q_n=tau^gamma+delta_n. V is initially stepwise defined; continuity is a proved assertion. The per-step formulas define a tuple of functions for any prescribed smooth mean-zero step state f_n, step start n*tau and positive tau, independently of whether f_n comes from a full trajectory. In that local use, any comparison state w may replace v(t_n) in delta_n=||f_n-w||_2 and q_n=tau^gamma+delta_n. Full concatenation, V on the whole time interval and e=v-V concern a full numerical trajectory and an exact solution as specified above."
    },
    {
      "op": "replace",
      "path": "/graph/inferences/@R001/argument/@S004/text",
      "value": "For arbitrary B>0 and two real mean-zero L2 initial data a0,b0 with norm sum <=B, apply E001's L2 existence and two-flow Lipschitz clauses at theta=0, T_s=T. Denote the resulting target-sign physical flows u_a,u_b, using the reflection already proved. The source twist and reflection preserve their normalized L2 distance, so sup_[0,T]||u_a-u_b||_2<=C(T,B)||a0-b0||_2. For any real mean-zero datum d0 in H^gamma, its real Fourier polynomial truncations tend to d0 in L2, and the same Lipschitz estimate shows convergence of their flows in C([0,T];L2). The limit agrees with the H^gamma flow by uniqueness. This proves the smooth-approximation convention and the full two-flow assertion."
    },
